# THE SECOND ENVIRONMENT ON ONE HOST. Nine variables and one network fix.
#
# ── A CORRECTION, BECAUSE THIS FILE SAID SOMETHING THAT WAS NOT TRUE ──────────
#
# This header used to read "Six variables, no second copy of anything", and item
# 2 below asserted the gateway files were made to follow `CF_NET_PREFIX`. Both
# were true of the CONSUMERS and false of the PRODUCER.
# `docker-compose.telemetry.yml` — the file that actually CREATES `edge`, `app`
# and `vault`, as `docker-compose.gateway.yml` says where it declares them
# external — hardcoded `cf-micro-edge`, `cf-micro-app` and `cf-micro-vault`, and
# named the LEGACY, STOPPED `stack_default` as the estate network.
#
# Measured, not inferred: with `CF_NET_PREFIX=cf-testnet CF_PROJECT=cf-testnet`
# set, `docker compose -f docker-compose.telemetry.yml config` emitted
# `cf-micro-app`, `cf-micro-edge` and `stack_default` — byte-identical to the
# unset run. So the six variables moved the testnet gateway to
# `cf-testnet-edge`/`-app` and left NOTHING able to create them, and the second
# environment could not start. That file is now parameterised; see its `networks:`
# block for the whole argument.
#
#
#   docker compose --env-file compose/testnet.env \
#     -f compose/docker-compose.estate.yml up -d
#
# ── WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY IT IS NOT A SECOND SET OF COMPOSE FILES ───────
#
# `.env.example` said, of COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: "Change only if you run two
# copies of this stack on one host, WHICH NOTHING HERE IS DESIGNED FOR." That was
# accurate, and three specific things made it so. All three are now variables
# with their existing values as defaults, so an unset environment renders these
# files byte-for-byte identically to before — verified with `docker compose
# config` against `git show HEAD:` for all three files, not assumed:
#
#   1. `name: cloudsforge-estate` was a LITERAL at docker-compose.estate.yml:64.
#      A top-level `name:` overrides COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME entirely, so setting
#      that variable did nothing at all — the documented escape hatch was already
#      closed by a line 6000 characters further down the same file. It is now
#      `${CF_PROJECT:-cloudsforge-estate}`.
#
#   2. The gateway files attach to `cf-micro-edge`, `cf-micro-app`,
#      `cf-micro-vault` and `cloudsforge-estate_default` by LITERAL NAME with
#      `external: true`. Two projects would therefore have shared one set of
#      networks — which is not isolation, it is one estate with two names, and
#      every service in each would have resolved the other's by container alias.
#      The gateway files now interpolate `${CF_NET_PREFIX:-cf-micro}` — and, as
#      the correction above records, `docker-compose.telemetry.yml` did NOT until
#      it was fixed alongside this note. It is the one that creates them.
#
#   3. Forty-five loopback debug ports (`127.0.0.1:41xx:4000`), the gateway's
#      443, and 9095/9096/9097 are all fixed. Only one process can bind a port;
#      the second project simply fails to start, which is at least loud.
#
# Volumes needed no change: compose namespaces them by project name, so
# `custody-keys` becomes `cf-testnet_custody-keys` automatically. That matters
# more than it looks — `custody-keys` holds every key the platform custodies, and
# two environments sharing it would be two ledgers spending one set of coins.
#
# ── WHY NOT KUBERNETES, IN ONE PARAGRAPH ──────────────────────────────────────
# The full argument is in micro-docs `ecosystem/26-public-deployment.md`. The
# short form: isolation here is a compose project, and the nine lines below are
# the whole mechanism — six that separate the projects, networks, ports and
# apexes, and three that separate the CHAIN, which none of the first six did.
# A single-node k3s would add an orchestrator to get the same two-namespace
# separation, and `deploy.replicas` is already illegal for anything this gateway
# routes by DNS name.

# The compose project. Sets container names, the default network
# (`cf-testnet_default`) and every volume prefix in one go.
CF_PROJECT=cf-testnet

# ── AND THE GATEWAY'S PROJECT, WHICH IS NOW THE SAME ONE (micro-org#257) ─────
#
# It used to be `cftestnet`, no hyphen, while the services it serves were in
# `cf-testnet`. ONE HYPHEN, and it cost two outages:
#
#   docker compose -p cf-testnet ps      <- did not list the gateway
#
# so the container every public `*-testnet` hostname depends on presented itself,
# to anybody auditing the host, as an unclaimed container belonging to no stack.
# Deleting it is the reasonable conclusion from what `docker ps` shows, and it
# was deleted twice in three days — 2026-08-05 and 2026-08-08 — each time with
# every service healthy and every testnet hostname answering 502, because
# cloudflared reports an unreachable origin as 502.
#
# It was also written down in exactly ONE executable place, the literal
# `-p cftestnet` in the Makefile, while THREE scripts derived their own:
#
#   estate-up.sh, gateway-reload.sh, estate-verify.sh
#     GW_PROJECT=${CF_GW_PROJECT:-${CF_PROJECT:-cloudsforge-estate}}
#
# On mainnet those agreed by coincidence — `CF_PROJECT` is `cloudsforge-estate`
# and so was the gateway's project. On testnet they did not: the three scripts
# looked in `cf-testnet` and the gateway was in `cftestnet`. Measured on the app
# host on 2026-08-10, against a gateway that was up, healthy, serving every
# public testnet hostname and answering the tunnel-origin check:
#
#   estate-verify.sh   "no running gateway in compose project 'cf-testnet'"
#                      -> "the gateway is running STALE configuration", which
#                         voids EVERY routing assertion after it
#   gateway-reload.sh  same, so the one command the failure tells you to run
#                      cannot find the thing it is meant to reload
#   estate-up.sh       worse than a false negative: it would `up -d gateway` in
#                      `cf-testnet`, i.e. build a SECOND testnet gateway beside
#                      the real one and race it for 9181 and 10443
#
# That last one is micro-org#257 exactly — a gateway in a project that is not the
# one its operators look in. Mainnet's half was fixed on 2026-08-10; this is
# testnet's, on 2026-08-11. The value now EQUALS `CF_PROJECT`, and the line stays
# anyway: the four entry points must read one value rather than derive four, and
# the next environment's may legitimately differ.
#
# ── WHAT THE RENAME COSTS, SO NOBODY IS SURPRISED BY IT ──────────────────────
#
# Nothing moves except a label. The two gateway compose files declare no
# top-level `volumes:` at all, and every network in them is `external:` with an
# explicit `name:` derived from `CF_NET_PREFIX`/`CF_PROJECT` — never from the
# compose project. So the rename creates no volume, orphans none, and re-attaches
# to the same three `cf-micro-*` networks and the same `cf-testnet_default`. It
# is a recreate of one container: a couple of seconds of 502 on the testnet
# hostnames, once.
#
# The one new hazard is the one mainnet already carries, recorded in
# `docs/releasing.md`: NEVER pass `--remove-orphans` to a `cf-testnet` invocation
# that does not also include the two gateway compose files. The gateway is in
# that project now and is not defined by `docker-compose.estate.yml`, so compose
# would offer to remove it. `release-deploy.sh` passes no such flag.
CF_GW_PROJECT=cf-testnet

# The three external networks the gateway attaches to. `external: true` means
# compose attaches and never creates, so this must match whatever brought them up
# for THIS project.
#
# WHAT BRINGS THEM UP IS `docker-compose.telemetry.yml`, NOT THE ESTATE. That
# matters here more than it looks, because the telemetry plane is deliberately
# SINGLE (see the section at the end of this file) — one Grafana showing both
# environments. A single telemetry project can only create ONE prefix's networks.
#
# So setting this variable is necessary and NOT sufficient, and the missing half
# is stated here rather than discovered: `cf-testnet-edge` and `cf-testnet-app`
# have to be created before the testnet gateway starts, and the shared telemetry
# containers have to be attached to them or Prometheus cannot scrape the testnet
# gateway at all. Creating them is one command and it is in the runbook beside
# this file; what must not happen is a second telemetry stack, which is the thing
# this file's last section spends a page arguing against.
CF_NET_PREFIX=cf-testnet

# The leading digit of the 45 loopback debug ports: 4100-4144 becomes 5100-5144.
# One character rather than 45 overrides, because a 45-entry override list is the
# hand-maintained list this repository has paid for four times.
#
# These are debug ports and nothing routes through them — the gateway reaches
# every service over the docker network by name. They exist so an operator can
# curl one service without going through TLS.
CF_PORT_BASE=5

# The gateway's browser port. Mainnet keeps 443; testnet takes 10443, chosen so
# it reads as its original with a `1` in front. Both are bound to 127.0.0.1
# ONLY — public exposure is the tunnel's job, and that stays true here.
CF_GATEWAY_PORT=10443

# 9095/9096/9097 (http, https-alt, traefik ping) become 9195/9196/9197.
CF_GW_PORT_BASE=91

# The gateway's env file. `env/traefik.testnet.env` sets CF_WEB_SUFFIX,
# CF_SITE_HOST and CF_API_HOST.
#
# THE APEX IS NO LONGER ONE OF THEM. Both environments are served on the zone
# `cloudsforge.online`; the environment lives inside the FIRST LABEL, as a suffix
# on the subdomain — `hub-testnet.cloudsforge.online`, apex surface
# `testnet.cloudsforge.online`. The old `hub.testnet.cloudsforge.online` shape
# needed no code change and was never reachable: Cloudflare's Universal SSL
# wildcard matches one label, so it failed the TLS handshake at the edge.
#
# See that file's header for the whole argument, and
# `scripts/check-apex-prefix.py` for the two collisions that would make a first
# label ambiguous.
CF_TRAEFIK_ENV=traefik.testnet

# ── THIS ENVIRONMENT'S HOSTNAMES, WHICH WERE CARRIED IN SHELL STATE ONLY ──────
#
# `docker-compose.estate.yml` interpolates these into identity's
# IDENTITY_HANDOFF_ORIGINS, into `BEACON_HANDOFF_ORIGIN` and into
# `FAUCET_CORS_ORIGINS`. `env/traefik.testnet.env` sets them too, but that file
# is the GATEWAY's env_file — compose never reads it, and compose is the half
# that writes the hand-off allowlist.
#
# So they were exported by hand at bring-up and no committed file said so. That
# is the same defect this file already records for `EMBER_RPC_URL` two blocks
# down ("previously carried in shell state only... restarting the estate from a
# clean shell would have silently taken the compose default"), and here the
# default is `.cloudsforge.localtest.me`: a testnet estate restarted from a clean
# shell would have written a hand-off allowlist for a laptop's loopback wildcard,
# and every sign-in on testnet would have completed at Hub and 403'd everywhere
# else. Measured on 2026-08-05: the running `cf-testnet-identity-1` carried the
# old two-label list, including the retired `foresight-admin.` origin, because
# nothing had recreated it since that shell.
#
# THEY MUST MATCH `env/traefik.testnet.env` EXACTLY. `scripts/estate-up.sh`
# refuses to start when they disagree — the gateway would route one set of
# hostnames while identity allowed another, and the symptom is a sign-in that
# works at Hub and fails silently at every other surface.
CF_WEB_APEX=cloudsforge.online
CF_WEB_SUFFIX=-testnet.cloudsforge.online
CF_SITE_HOST=testnet.cloudsforge.online

# ── THE CHAIN, WHICH IS THE PART THAT WAS NOT ISOLATED AT ALL ─────────────────
#
# Everything above separates projects, networks, ports and apexes. None of it
# separated the CHAIN, and until these three lines the two environments were two
# independent chains sharing chain id 7412 — separate volumes, separate genesis,
# no peering, one identity. A transaction signed for one was replayable on the
# other, because EIP-155 binds a signature to the chain id and to nothing else.
#
# Mainnet now runs `hearth` / 7411 (`hearth/node/src/params.js:37-38`); this
# environment keeps `hearth-testnet` / 7412, which is what it always was.
#
# `CF_EMBER_NETWORK` picks `env/chain.testnet.env`, which carries the indexer's
# scope and RPC variable — two of whose NAMES contain the network, which is why
# they are in a file whose path is interpolated rather than in the compose file.
CF_EMBER_NETWORK=testnet

# THIS ENVIRONMENT'S OWN SEED, and it was previously carried in shell state only.
# The running `cf-testnet` estate had `EMBER_RPC_URL` pointing at 8745, but no
# committed file said so: it had been exported by hand at bring-up. Restarting
# the estate from a clean shell would have silently taken the compose default of
# 8545 — THE MAINNET SEED — and pointed the testnet estate's indexer, faucet and
# foresight at the other environment's chain, which is the same class of defect
# `env/traefik.testnet.env` was fixed for. Ports from
# `docker-compose.hearth-seed-testnet.yml`.
#
# THE HOST IS A VARIABLE AND THE PORT IS NOT, which is the same division the
# gateway's upstreams make in `env/traefik.testnet.env`. `CF_CHAIN_HOST` moved on
# 2026-08-10 when the app stack left the machine the seeds run on
# (micro-org#338); the PORT is what keeps this environment off the mainnet seed,
# so it stays a literal here where it can be read and argued about.
#
# ── WHERE CF_CHAIN_HOST HAS TO COME FROM, AND WHERE IT MUST NOT ──────────────
#
# Compose DOES interpolate `--env-file` values — measured on the app host with
# compose v2.40.3, both the substitution and the `:-` fallback — but it does it in
# FILE ORDER, and every command in this repo passes this file FIRST and
# `estate/tokens.testnet.env` LAST. So setting CF_CHAIN_HOST in the tokens file
# would leave this line already resolved to the default, and the estate would
# quietly point at a `host.docker.internal` that has no chain on it.
#
# Put it in the ENVIRONMENT instead — `deploy/.env`, which `up.sh` sources with
# `set -a` before it calls compose. The environment is consulted for every file
# regardless of order, which makes this immune to the trap rather than merely
# arranged around it. `env/traefik.testnet.env` has no such constraint: it is an
# `env_file:` on the gateway service and is resolved against the whole merged
# project environment, tokens included.
EMBER_RPC_URL=http://${CF_CHAIN_HOST:-host.docker.internal}:8745

# WHAT ADMITS THE FAUCET. `docker-compose.estate.yml` puts `faucet` and
# `faucet-migrate` behind the `ember-testnet` profile because
# `faucet/src/env.ts:63` fixes the service to the testnet at COMPILE time and
# `index.ts:108-120` exits on any node that is not 7412 — so it can only ever run
# here. Compose can add a service for a profile but cannot remove one, so the
# profile is on the faucet and the opt-in is this line.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=ember-testnet

# ── AND WHAT THE LINE ABOVE DOES *NOT* ADMIT, SAID OUT LOUD (micro-org#406) ───
#
# The mirror image of the line above: it tells the pool CONSOLE about a service
# this file deliberately does not admit.
#
# `pool` and `pool-migrate` are behind the `pool` profile, which is not named
# above, so they are never created here. That is correct and permanent —
# `pool/src/env.ts` refuses a `POOL_NETWORK` outside mainnet|testnet,
# `pool/src/template.ts` refuses to build a template when the node's own
# `getblockchaininfo` chain disagrees with it, and every chain in `POOL_CHAINS`
# needs a node URL AND a payout address or the process exits at boot. A pool
# container on this estate would never become healthy and would take the
# deploy's `--wait` down with it.
#
# `pool-web` has no profile, on purpose, so that the page explaining the absence
# is reachable when the service is not. MEASURED 2026-08-11: it explained
# nothing. Traefik has no backend for `/v1` here, so it answered 502, and all
# three console pages rendered "The pool did not answer" with a **Try again**
# button that could never succeed — a deliberate absence reported as a fault, on
# a network behaving exactly as this file intends.
#
# This is the sentence that turns those three error pages into one explanation
# with a working link to the pool that does exist. It also stops the requests:
# the console makes no `/v1` call at all once it has been told. `mainnet.env`
# sets the opposite value beside its own `COMPOSE_PROFILES`, where the two can be
# read together.
#
# ONE VARIABLE, TWO CONTAINERS. `hub-web` reads it as well, and its half of the
# defect was the worse one: `hub-testnet.<apex>/mine` turned the same 502 into a
# FULL-PAGE failure, so EMBER — mined in the browser against hearth, nothing to
# do with micro-pool — was unreachable behind an error about micro-pool, on the
# one network where EMBER is the only thing a browser can usefully mine. Both
# services take the value from here because it is one fact about this estate;
# `docker-compose.estate.yml` argues each service's half at its own block.
POOL_API_PRESENCE=absent

# ── NOT SET HERE, DELIBERATELY: the telemetry plane ───────────────────────────
#
# There is ONE observability stack for both environments, not two. It stays in
# the `cfmicro` project on its existing ports, and both estates ship to it.
#
# The argument: Prometheus, Grafana, Loki and Tempo cost ~770 MiB and buy nothing
# when duplicated — you want ONE Grafana showing both environments side by side,
# with an `env` label to tell them apart, because the question an operator
# actually asks during an incident is "is this happening on both?". Two Grafanas
# cannot answer it. Duplicating them would add ~770 MiB to a box being specified
# at 32 GB for other reasons.
#
# The cost, stated: a scrape config that fails to distinguish the two
# environments merges their metrics, and a merged EMBER supply figure across a
# real chain and a test one is exactly the kind of number that ends up in a
# reconciliation alarm. The `env` label is therefore load-bearing and belongs in
# prometheus/targets/, not here.

# ── WHERE THE BACKUP RUNNER WRITES, AND WHAT IT READS (micro-org#434) ─────────
#
# The argument is written out in `mainnet.env`; only the values differ. The
# destination is a SEPARATE directory rather than a shared one with a `testnet/`
# child, because `BACKUP_ROOT` is the same `/backups` in both containers and the
# runner's own cross-environment refusal is the last thing that should be asked
# to compensate for two estates writing into one tree.
#
# No testnet backup runner is up at the time of writing. These are here so that
# bringing one up is a decision rather than an archaeology exercise.
CF_BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT=testnet
CF_BACKUP_DESTINATION=/home/savvaniss/cloudsforge-backups-testnet
CF_MINER_KEYS=/home/savvaniss/dev/cloudsforge/miner-keys

# `CF_BACKUP_SECURITY_OPT=no-new-privileges:false` was here from micro-org#329
# until 2026-08-12. It was a fact about the OLD host's canonical-snap docker, not
# about this environment. This host is Docker Desktop 29.1.2, the flag works on
# it (measured 2026-08-12), and the compose default is the hardened `true`.

# ── TESTNET TRUSTS THE MAINNET IDENTITY (micro-org#459, the combined view) ─────────────────────
#
# One identity serves both estates: testnet services verify tokens against the mainnet JWKS and
# exchange their service credentials at the mainnet identity, both through the public tunnel
# origin. The testnet identity retires once this holds. Safety is the net claim, not distance —
# a testnet service token names its estate and every mainnet verifier refuses it.
CF_IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=https://nimbus.cloudsforge.online/.well-known/jwks.json
CF_IDENTITY_URL=https://nimbus.cloudsforge.online
